.TH velinfo "" "Command"
.SH NAME
velinfo \- measure, find and list a vellum drawing set
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvelinfo \-len\fR \fIfile.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvelinfo \-where\fR \fIstring file.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvelinfo \-symsheet\fR \fIlib.sym\fR > \fIcard.d\fR
.br
\fBvelinfo \-book\fR \fIsheet.d ...\fR > \fIcontents.d\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
velinfo -- measure, find and list a vellum drawing set
Usage:	velinfo -len file.d ...		the takeoff: lengths, areas
	velinfo -where str file.d ...	the set-wide Find
	velinfo -symsheet lib.sym	a library's reference card
	velinfo -book sheet.d ...	the set's contents page
	-len and -where answer in prose; -symsheet and -book
	answer with an ordinary DRAWING.  - is standard input.
.HE
A drawing set lives for years, and the questions the shop asks about
it \- what it takes, where anything is, what a library holds and what
the set contains \-
.B velinfo
answers.
These are the shell's questions, not the window's, and their natural
asker is
.BR make (1).
.PP
Two of the four answer in prose on standard output and two answer with
an ordinary DRAWING \- which is the point: a drawing is the format the
rest of the suite already prints, so a generated card goes to paper
with no new backend and no temporary file.
The file name
.B \-
is standard input.
.PP
.B \-len
.br
The TAKEOFF.
Wires, connectors and polylines are measured in sheet units, per NET
where the conductors have names and per LAYER otherwise, with a set
total.
H-V routes measure both legs; diagonals measure an integer square
root.
.PP
A closed FILLED polyline reports AREA instead \- integer shoelace,
halved, with the odd half shown as ``.5'' \- because sheet metal is
ordered by area the way cable is ordered by length.
.PP
A set whose sheets disagree about the
.B U
unit is refused in one line rather than adding millimetres to inches.
(That disagreement is also a
.B velcheck
finding, which is where a build should catch it.)
.PP
.B \-where
.br
The set-wide Find: a case-blind substring over designators, values,
text and net names \- the editor's Find contract re-stated over
FILES \- one line per hit with the grid position.
The EXIT STATUS is the hit count.
.PP
Zero hits exits 0 silently, so the INVERTED use is free:
.B "velinfo -where TODO *.d"
in a
.B check:
rule fails the build while a sheet still carries a TODO note.
.PP
.B \-symsheet
.br
The library ON PAPER: a grid of every stencil in ONE
.B .sym
file with its code, designator prefix and pin types, emitted as an
ordinary drawing, so the shop's binder fills itself from make:
.DS
.ta 8n
	velinfo -symsheet pid.sym | velplot -T ps -fit -
.DE
The card names its stencils by CODE like any drawing, so whatever
reads it back must have that library loaded: put a library of your own
in /usr/vellum/etc/libs first.
.PP
.B \-book
.br
The set as a DOCUMENT: an ordinary drawing listing the sheets given \-
number, file name, and the sheet's own title, which it takes from the
first text object on the FRAME layer (where the frame stamp puts
.BR $F ,
so a stamped set titles itself), falling back to the largest text on
the sheet and then to the file name.
.PP
It is
.B .d
out, so it prints on every device,
.B velplot \-tile s
if it has to, and opens in the editor when somebody wants to fix a
title by hand:
.DS
.ta 8n
	velinfo -book amp?.d > contents.d
	velplot -T ps contents.d amp?.d | lpr
.DE
The sheets stay FILES.
A book is a contents drawing plus a shell glob in the right order, not
a container format.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B vellum ,
.B velnet ,
.B velcheck ,
.B velplot ,
.B velsym ,
.B make
.SH NOTES
.B \-len
measures the nets the same union-find found for
.BR velnet ,
so a run length and a netlist can never disagree.
